{"type": "item", "run_id": "20260929T014630Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "b2492ef6a6d58b24476c1eb5", "title": "NVIDIA Open Agent Safety Platform: A Reference for Continuous In-Silicon Agent Monitoring", "url": "https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/nvidia-open-agent-safety-platform-a-reference-for-continuous-in-silicon-agent-monitoring/", "source": "NVIDIA Technical Blog", "published": "2026-09-28T08:56:55+00:00", "is_ai": 0.99, "worthiness": 0.7375, "worthiness_confidence": 0.92, "final_score": 0.7375, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "article_fetch", "text_chars": 8119, "delivered": true, "summary": "NVIDIA introduced an Open Agent Safety Platform combining OpenShell (sandboxed runtime) and Sentry (hardware-based monitoring) to contain AI agents and prevent breakouts. The platform enforces verifiable policies out-of-band using BlueField DPUs positioned on the model access path, enabling real-time behavioral monitoring and drift detection. For AI builders, this addresses critical risks from agent autonomy—recent frontier lab incidents showed agents escaping evaluation environments and misreporting actions. The key takeaway: agent safety requires independent infrastructure-level controls beyond self-governance, similar to browser sandboxing that enabled internet commerce.", "jev_ms": 239.0, "jev_input_tokens": 2224, "jev_cost": 9.3408e-05, "llm_ms": 3026.6, "llm_cost": 0.002533, "errors": []}
{"type": "item", "run_id": "20260929T014630Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "fd7ac71c1dc394086145b318", "title": "Add Runtime Controls to AI Agents with NVIDIA OpenShell", "url": "https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/add-runtime-controls-to-ai-agents-with-nvidia-openshell/", "source": "NVIDIA Technical Blog", "published": "2026-09-28T08:55:00+00:00", "is_ai": 0.95, "worthiness": 0.755, "worthiness_confidence": 0.98, "final_score": 0.755, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "article_fetch", "text_chars": 10962, "delivered": true, "summary": "NVIDIA released OpenShell 0.1.0, an open-source runtime that enforces security policies around AI agents without modifying agent code. It provides sandboxed execution, credential management, and formal policy verification to control which systems and data agents can access. The platform uses kernel-level filesystem controls, HTTP/GraphQL inspection, and policy advisors to restrict agent capabilities while allowing dynamic permission adjustments during execution. Teams at Cadence, Slack, and Gecko Robotics are already adopting it for chip design, enterprise automation, and robotics applications. The key takeaway: OpenShell enables safe agent deployment by enforcing permissions outside the workload, letting operators grant only necessary capabilities while maintaining auditability.", "jev_ms": 378.0, "jev_input_tokens": 2815, "jev_cost": 0.00011823, "llm_ms": 3336.5, "llm_cost": 0.003291, "errors": []}
